## 2. Seven powerful lessons from the book

1\. Fill your five buckets in the right order

Bartlett describes five assets: knowledge, skills, network, resources, and reputation.

Build knowledge and skills first. They help you create opportunities, earn resources, develop relationships, and establish a reputation.

2\. Teach what you learn

Explaining a concept to someone else exposes gaps in your understanding and strengthens your knowledge.

Try it: After studying a programming topic, explain it without looking at your notes.

3\. Your story influences your behaviour

If you repeatedly tell yourself that you're incapable, you may avoid the very experiences that could help you improve.

Replace fixed labels with specific, changeable observations: instead of “I'm bad at coding,” say “I need more practice with recursion.”

4\. Fail, learn, and improve

Avoiding every possible failure can prevent progress. Take thoughtful risks, learn from the result, and improve your next attempt.

5\. Ask “Who?” instead of always asking “How?”

When facing a difficult task, consider whether someone with the right expertise can help you solve it more effectively.

This is especially useful in teamwork, entrepreneurship, and complex projects.

6\. Make progress visible

Large goals can feel overwhelming. Break them into measurable milestones so you can recognize improvement and adjust your approach.

7\. Learning never ends

Success isn't the end of development. Keep updating your knowledge, learning from others, and questioning what you think you already know.

